# HTSanalyzeR2 0.99.19

## Algorithms, reproducibility and legacy removals

* `analyze()` on an `NWA` object no longer aborts on networks where the
  profitable negative nodes are pairwise non-adjacent. BioNet's FastHeinz
  solver takes the minimum spanning tree of an edgeless subgraph internally and
  aborts there under igraph 2.x; `identifySubnetwork()` keeps using FastHeinz by
  default and, only for that failure, solves the same problem exactly with a
  mixed-integer linear program (`lpSolve`). The fallback is exact rather than a
  heuristic, so it never returns a lower-scoring module than FastHeinz would.
  Instances that are too large to solve within the configured limits stop with a
  message naming the option to raise, instead of failing inside igraph.
* Network analysis is now reproducible. BioNet draws the starting values of the
  beta-uniform mixture fit with `runif()` and keeps the last successful fit
  rather than the best one, so the identified subnetwork changed between runs
  (118, 119 and 121 nodes were observed for one fixed input). `fitBumModelStable()`
  searches a fixed set of seeds, keeps the best fit by negative log-likelihood,
  and leaves the caller's random number stream untouched.
* The screen statistics no longer need `cellHTS2`, which has been removed from
  Bioconductor. `screenStatTests()` performs the one-sample and two-sample
  t-test, Mann-Whitney and Rank Product tests on a plain matrix, an annotation
  vector and a sample/control labelling; `cellHTS2OutputStatTests()` is now a
  thin adapter over it for users who still have cellHTS objects. Results were
  verified to be identical to the previous implementation across all test and
  alternative combinations.
* Fixed a regression introduced during the modernization work: the construct
  identifiers were lost from the row names of the screen statistics result.
* The GSEA permutation engine is unchanged, but the profile is now dominated by
  the permutations themselves (69% of the time in `sample.int`), which is
  inherent to the method. `GSEA.by = "fgsea"` delegates to the maintained
  implementation and is about an order of magnitude faster; it is documented in
  the README and covered by tests.

## Performance

* `extractEnrichMap()` builds the Jaccard similarity between all pairs of gene
  sets from a sparse incidence matrix (`Matrix::tcrossprod`) instead of a
  double loop calling `intersect()`/`union()` per pair. On 367 gene sets this
  reduced the step from 1.98s to 0.04s — about 50x — with bit-identical output.
* `duplicateRemover()` groups positions once instead of rescanning the name
  vector for every gene, which removes a quadratic scan. On a 20000-element
  gene list this is 12-16x faster with identical output.
* `analyze(GSEA.by = "fgsea")` calls `fgsea::fgseaSimple()` directly instead of
  the deprecated `fgsea(nperm=)` branch, so it no longer warns on every run, and
  it hands the registered BiocParallel backend to fgsea.
* The completion messages of `analyze()` now honour `verbose = FALSE`.

## Licence

* The package is now distributed under the Apache License 2.0. The
  previously bundled Sigma/linkurious.js copy was GPLv3 and has been removed.

## Interactive graphs

* New `saveNetwork()` writes an enrichment map or subnetwork to a
  self-contained interactive HTML file or, with the optional `webshot2`
  package, to a publication-ready PNG from a script — no manual clicking in
  the report (issue #4).
* `viewEnrichMap()` and `viewSubNet()` are rendered with **visNetwork**
  (vis.js) instead of the unmaintained Sigma/linkurious.js and jQuery bundle.
  Node sizes, the dual positive/negative colour scales, the legend, the node
  labels and the time-series updates behave as before; hover details and PNG
  export are new.
* Widgets can be saved as standalone interactive HTML with
  `htmlwidgets::saveWidget()`.

## Report

* The Shiny report is built with **bslib** (Bootstrap 5). The settings panel is
  now standard Shiny inputs with one namespaced panel per graph, replacing the
  hand-written HTML/ionRangeSlider sidebar.
* `reportAll()` stores its results through the new `prepareReport()` helper,
  which assembles a report directory without launching the application.
* The one-shot network render no longer relies on an observer driven by a
  constant expression.

## Dependencies

* `cellHTS2OutputStatTests()` reports an actionable message when `cellHTS2` is
  missing. `cellHTS2` has been removed from Bioconductor, so this legacy path
  is optional and is never needed to install the package (issue #3).
* New optional `webshot2` in `Suggests` for PNG export.
* Removed `foreach`, `doParallel`, `stringr`, `htmlwidgets` and
  `shinydashboard`; added `visNetwork` and `bslib`.
* `RankProd` moved from `Imports` to `Suggests`: the Rank Product test now
  reports a clear message when the optional package is missing.
* Parallel execution now uses `BiocParallel` throughout, including the
  `HTSanalyzeR2Pipe()` entry point.
* `MSigDBGeneSets()` uses the current `msigdbr(collection=, subcollection=)`
  interface, and BioGRID downloads follow the current `Latest-Release` archive.
* Fixed `igraph` 2.x breakage: `as_data_frame(x, "edge")` is now
  `as_data_frame(x, "edges")`, which had made `viewEnrichMap()` and
  `viewSubNet()` fail outright.

